    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Thanks for the info on eccentric loading. We've also been debating whether interlocking patterns (like a brick pattern) are better for stability in the lower levels, or if just stacking them straight up is safer since we have to worry about the weight shifting during forklift movement. What's your take on interlocking vs. column stacking for high-density setups?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Interlocking patterns, like a brick pattern, are generally superior for stability, especially when dealing with uneven or heavy loads in lower levels. They create a much stronger, more cohesive unit, preventing individual pallets from shifting laterally under the stress of forklift maneuvering. While straight stacking is simpler, it relies entirely on the friction and integrity of the pallet underneath, which is less robust against dynamic forces. For high-density setups, the interlocking method distributes the load forces more evenly across the entire stack.",
      "source": "target",
